### Actual behavior

Description
When LLMs output LaTeX equations in chat responses, the backslashes and angle brackets are often HTML-escaped, causing KaTeX to not render them. For example:
\\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\) appears as raw text \\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\) instead of being rendered as Δ = b² − 4ac
&lt; 0 appears as &lt; 0 instead of < 0
This happens because preprocessLaTeX() in web/src/utils/chat.ts expects \( / \[ as delimiters (single backslash), but many LLMs (including DeepSeek V4 Flash, Qwen3-MAX) return \\( / \\[ (double backslash) in the response text. The angle brackets < and > are also HTML-escaped to &lt; and &gt; in certain rendering paths.

<img width="1121" height="478" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1c9209d0-640b-44f7-873e-1df3066f6e02" />

### Expected behavior

LaTeX equations should render correctly via KaTeX. The output should look like:
Δ = (−5)² − 4 × 2 × 6 = −23 < 0

### Steps to reproduce

```Markdown
Ask a math question in chat mode, e.g. "Calculate the discriminant of 2x² − 5x + 6 = 0"
LLM responds with LaTeX like \\(\\Delta = (-5)^2 - 4 \\times 2 \\times 6 = -23\\)
The equation shows as raw text instead of being rendered by KaTeX
```

### Additional information

Root Cause
The preprocessLaTeX function in web/src/utils/chat.ts uses regex patterns to match \(...\) and \[...\]:
const BLOCK_MATH_RE = /\\\[([\s\S]*?)(?<![a-zA-Z])\\\]/g;

const INLINE_MATH_RE = /\\\(([\s\S]*?)(?<![a-zA-Z])\\)/g;

These patterns match \[ / \( (escaped brackets in the text), but LLMs often return \\[ / \\( (where the backslash itself is escaped as \\). The HTML entities &lt; and &gt; used in mathematical comparisons are also not decoded.
Suggested Fix
In web/src/utils/chat.ts, add a preprocessing step to normalize double backslashes and decode HTML entities before the existing LaTeX delimiter conversion:
